"","caption":"Wavetrain #1","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Garrick Taylor","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884600","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884600"}},{"id":"884601","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887309","description":"","caption":"Wavetrain #2","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Garrick Taylor","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884601","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884601"}},{"id":"884602","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887310","description":"","caption":"Wavetrain #3","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Garrick Taylor","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884602","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884602"}},{"id":"884603","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887311","description":"","caption":"Around the Corner","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Garrick Taylor","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884603","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884603"}},{"id":"6130","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5046","description":"","caption":"Playing on Twelvemile","poi_name":"","subject":"Eric Paysen","author":"Will Reeves","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6130","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6130"}},{"id":"4733","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5046","description":"","caption":"Riverwide Hole","poi_name":"","subject":"Kevin Miller","author":"Will Reeves","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/4733","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/4733"}},{"id":"5243","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4942","description":"","caption":"Loop","poi_name":"Lay Bridge","subject":"Wade Harrison","author":"Wade Harrison","poi_id":"103444","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5243","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5243"}},{"id":"884592","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887300","description":"","caption":"Boogie Part 2","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Garrick Taylor","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884592","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884592"}},{"id":"884593","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887301","description":"","caption":"Scout Hut #1","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Garrick Taylor","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884593","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884593"}}],"detail":{"id":"1709","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"Written with input from multiple sources including Ethan Talley and Garrick Taylor\n\n**The dams have been removed on this section!**\n\n**The 12 Mile will be in a state of flux for some time as large sediment loads move downstream.  Conditions will change, particularly during high water events.  Quite a few trees are in play in a number of rapids, and there's potential for more to fall in as the banks stabilize.  Use caution when paddling, especially during the first few high water events.**\n\nFrom SC 137 to the Lay Bridge there is just about 101 feet of gradient in the 12 Mile over 1.8 miles.\n\nRapids are rated for average flows (~500 cfs), so raise ratings by one notch when paddling high water, or lower them a bit for low water.  The very bottom of the run can be run as low as 100 cfs by hiking up the road from the takeout and then down to the river.\n\n**Note on shuttle and parking:**\n\nThis river’s takeout and put in are very close to residential and high-traffic roads. Be aware as you change and set shuttle/ run laps. Be kind and courteous to the locals and speak whenever possible. The fishermen are usually extremely friendly and very curious about paddle sports. That being said, don’t ruin our reputation here. We could easily start running into vandalism and theft in this area if we don’t keep a good rapport with the locals.\n\nThere is a new put-in parking area on upstream river right.  The previous put-in used on upstream river left is now posted as a tow zone due to people blocking the gate there.  Don't park there.  The new parking area is owned by Easley Utilities and leased for use as a put-in.  Please park here and keep valuables locked out of sight to discourage break-ins (there haven't been any - let's keep it that way).  There is a private drive there.  DO NOT drive up this driveway as you will block the landowners that live at the end of that road.  You can walk your boat up the driveway to access a put-in location just below the dam.  DO NOT walk past the \"Keep Out\" sign or you will anger the landowners.  There is an old drive before the \"keep out\" sign on the right that goes down to the river just below the dam.  Put-in here or put-in on river left at the bridge after parking your car.\n\nThe takeout is at Lay Bridge and parking is roadside on both sides of the bridge.  Park with all tires off of the road and be courteous.\n\n**A note from the about flows and river characteristics:**\n\nThis river is extremely unique. It takes some time to fill up when the rains come, but seems to hold at around 600 cfs for a few days after significant rainfall. This river does merit some special notes about levels. First, 600 cfs when the water is rising seems to be drastically different than 600 cfs when the water is falling. This is likely due to that fact that the gauge is several miles upstream and several tributaries join below the gauge. That means you should know that going into this trip, your experience could be very different than the last time you were there. Study the flow charts and know when the river is rising or falling. Also, at levels above 1000 cfs, this river takes on a completely different life. Imagine the Ocoee being constricted into a 50 foot-wide walled gorge. Large wavetrains with massive downstream V’s makes you feel like you’re on the Gauley. Most holes wash out, but the big ones just get bigger. Be careful, but enjoy this big water feel that happens only a few times a year.","averageGradient":"56","maxGradient":"0","length":"1.9","correlations":[{"reachID":"1709","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"100","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"2000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"TWELVEMILE CREEK NEAR LIBERTY, SC","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"02186000","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"59","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"1.72","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"34.8015056","longitude":"-82.7484771"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/02186000","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/02186000/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":"Previous Flowbands: \nHigh Runnable (R6-R9): 300 - 1000 cfs Adjusted Difficulty: IIplusstandoutIII Comment: Many spots to play in, some to stay out of.\nMedium Runnable (R4-R6): 200 - 300 cfs Adjusted Difficulty: ItoII Comment: Optimum levels for rodeo hole.\nLow Runnable Range (R0-R5): 100 - 200 cfs Adjusted Difficulty: ItoII Comment: ELF\nHigh Runnable (R6-R9): 1000 - 2000 cfs Adjusted Difficulty: IIIplus Comment: Approaching flood stage, use caution.\n\nMigration Errors: \nMore than 3 ranges associated","comment":null,"status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"59","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["101779","2380","2381","1833","WILQqr4jdPqfDEw12ka7E"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-82.771687,34.774586],[-82.771822,34.774305],[-82.771822,34.774305],[-82.772166,34.774085],[-82.772648,34.774067],[-82.773174,34.774191],[-82.773539,34.774438],[-82.773678,34.774684],[-82.773743,34.775002],[-82.77385,34.775151],[-82.775255,34.775645],[-82.775255,34.775645],[-82.775738,34.775592],[-82.776253,34.775398],[-82.776875,34.775354],[-82.777594,34.775248],[-82.777991,34.775169],[-82.778378,34.775037],[-82.778893,34.77479],[-82.779257,34.774464],[-82.779257,34.774464],[-82.77944,34.774305],[-82.77974,34.773724],[-82.779858,34.773221],[-82.779858,34.77301],[-82.779858,34.77301],[-82.779858,34.772604],[-82.779837,34.772164],[-82.779837,34.772164],[-82.779901,34.772058],[-82.78003,34.771961],[-82.780201,34.771873],[-82.780337,34.771855],[-82.780337,34.771855],[-82.780867,34.771785],[-82.78194,34.77175],[-82.782905,34.77152],[-82.784278,34.771283],[-82.784278,34.771283],[-82.784793,34.771053],[-82.785856,34.770295],[-82.785856,34.770295],[-82.786656,34.769842],[-82.786656,34.769842],[-82.787132,34.769573],[-82.788184,34.768674],[-82.789117,34.768066],[-82.789536,34.767687],[-82.789654,34.767548],[-82.789654,34.767548],[-82.79004,34.767096],[-82.790619,34.766576],[-82.791317,34.766136],[-82.791317,34.766136],[-82.792509,34.765514],[-82.792509,34.765514],[-82.792754,34.765386],[-82.792969,34.765237],[-82.793173,34.765078],[-82.793494,34.764769],[-82.793494,34.764769],[-82.793634,34.764531],[